Date: Wed, 11 Aug 93 09:50:18 EDT
From: Brian.Hawthorne@east.sun.com (Brian Holt Hawthorne - SunSelect Engineering)
Subject: ECON: Side note on investment thread...

Actually, when I ran the numbers, I found that the 30 year financing wins
because of tax effects (home mortgage interest deduction). I went with 5 year
money to get a lower rate (i.e., 30 year mortgage with balloon payment
in 5 years). This gives me low monthly payment, most of which is interest.

Factoring in the taxes, and assuming I can earn at least 4% after taxes
with the money saved, I'm better off keeping my debt high, and investing the
extra instead of paying off principal. 

I also refinance at zero points, no closing costs, so I can do it 
frequently if rates keep going down (I've done it twice in the last 18 months).
I take a bit of a hit in the rate (less than 1/4%) for the lack of 
closing costs, but the added flexibility makes it worth it.

