Date: Wed, 11 Aug 1993 10:51:55 -0400
From: "Perry E. Metzger" <pmetzger@lehman.com>
Subject: Iridum Contract Announce 

Duncan Frissell says:
> Standing in a formerly top secret satellite test bay at Lockheed Missiles 
> and Spacecraft, Motorola officials yesterday announced the award to 
> Lockheed of a $700 million contract to build satellites for the proposed 
> Iridium system of LEO communications satellites which will offer hand-held 
> telephone communications from any point on earth to any other point.  
> (Think of it as a cellphone system in which the *users* are stationary and 
> the cell sites move.)  Total system cost projected in the $4 billion 
> range.  Service by 1999 if they can raise the money.  Maybe they should 
> hit Perry up for an investment <G>.

This project and the nearly complete English Channel tunnel show an
interesting thing, of course, that the statists claim to be
impossible: multi billion dollar projects can and are routinely
undertaken by the private sector without subsidy.

> In their core coding, Arpanet and its successor the Internetworking 
> Protocol (IP) were designed to operate even if some (many!) of their nodes 
> had been slagged by nuclear detonations.

You have this slightly wrong. The "Arpanet" during its existance ran
NCP until 1983 or so, and TCP/IP from then until it was decomissioned
around 1987. To my knowledge, the Arpanet was never made deliberately
nuclear-hard -- certainly the IMPs, the routers that made up the early
net, were made of ordinary non-EMP or radiation hard components. The
net could try to route around disruptions -- but then again, all nets
try to do that.

> This being the case, the nets 
> can have little to fear from ordinary political interference.  Endgame.

The internet is designed to be robust around routing failures, but
quite frankly it would not be a challenge to shut down.  Cut a few
lines in Reston VA and a couple of other places and the internet qua
internet would be down for the count. I could likely name the links in
question. Things are sturdy, but hardly sabotage proof. Yet.

Perry



