In <9308111343.AA20980@sea.East.Sun.COM>, Brian Holt Hawthorne - SunSelect Engineering writes:
|> > When the
|> > wholesale price of salted rice cakes is higher in Miami than in Boise, the
|> > redistributors buy the stuff in Boise in quanitity and resell it in Miami,
|> > undercutting the mainstream distributors and making a profit. 
|> 
|> I'll have to get a copy of the Forbes article (do you have the date?).
|> The spread would have to be pretty large to make up for transportation
|> costs.

Sometimes per-unit transportation costs can run very low, as in small
highly stackable items.

An example of an arbitrage opportunity that stays open for a long
time, but is harder to exploit, is antiques. There are people who go
to auctions in New England, where the supply of antiques is naturally
greater, and run truckloads of stuff to dealers in the Sunbelt; but I
have the impression that these are small specialist operations.

The "Dog Food" scenario exploits more highly transient spreads, right?
so the real-time information is key. And you can get beat to the punch
and drop a bundle on any given trade. Sic transit commoditas ;-)

