Date: Wed, 11 Aug 93 11:54:06 EDT
From: Brian.Hawthorne@east.sun.com (Brian Holt Hawthorne - SunSelect Engineering)
Subject: ECON: Side note on investment thread...

> First, what figures do you use when computing tax savings? I assume you're
> not using the deduction amount, and instead the actual tax savings. Can
> anyone else also run the numbers to do a verification of Brian's results?

Basically, I started with my (then) current scenario: a 15 year mortgage, 
approximately $200/month going into savings/investment, and marginal tax 
rate of 33%.

I assumed that we will be moving within 5 years, and then tried different
scenarios: 30-year fixed, 30/5, a new 15-year fixed, etc.

In each case, any additional money saved went into investment/savings.
I then calculated delta net worth, based on equity in the house (i.e., principal
paid off), total investment balance, etc., at 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5 years.

For the tax effects, I assumed that mortgage interest remains fully deductible,
thus I could offset the interest payments by the tax benefit at the 
marginal 33% rate. I assumed all investments in taxable instruments, and
applied the marginal 33% rate for those as well.

I've got an Excel spreadsheet somewhere that I'll try to dig up. The
results varied based on available mortgage and investment interest rates, 
but basically showed the 15 year falling behind after a couple of years
in my case.

> Also, what kind of balloon payment in 5 years?

This is a straight 30 year mortgage with a 5 year ballon note rider. 
Basically, if I still hold the mortgage at the end of 5 years, I 
have to pay the remaining principal in full. (Or refinance, of course). 

