Date:    Wed, 11 Aug 93 13:42 EDT
From: "Andrew I Cohen" <UANDCOH@UNCMVS.OIT.UNC.EDU>
Subject: LOGIC: Fallacies list - reference (was: Modern English Usage)

Anton Sherwood recently mentioned (#93-8-359) that someone had asked for
for a list of fallacies and their names and definitions.  I don't
remember that request, but I have one good reference:

  Check out David Kelly's, _The Art Of Reasoning_  Both the original
(1988) and the expanded version with symbolic logic (1990) give a
few chapters to a discussion of fallacies.  The discussion is clear,
accessible, and offers good, "modern-day" applications.  In fact,
the whole book is excellent.  It's intended as a textbook for
courses in critical reasoning or introductory logic (I used it
for my own Intro to Logic course I taught about two years back).  For
folks who need a refresher in critical thinking skills, the book is
quite nice.  The examples always bring abstractions down to
concretes, and Kelley does a good job of showing how critical
thinking skills (and even techniques in propositional and predicate
logic) can apply in helping to sort through some meaningful issues.

  And for the teachers in the crowd:  the text is only $30.00.  That's
  much cheaper (and much better written) than the exorbitant
  $50.00+ for the Copi text (is he up to a 9th edition yet?). The
  publishers also provide an instructor's manual, and there's software
  tutorials available for the students.

  No, I am not a paid spokesman for W.W. Norton & Co.

Brush and floss regularly,

  Andrew Cohen
  uandcoh@uncmvs.oit.unc.edu

