This trick of plotting investments in terms of %ROI and %STDEV is a
well-known method, part of what goes under the name "portfolio theory", and
basic to theoretical finance.  The mean and variance of any number of
investments are available from published sources.  I learned about all this
stuff from a standard textbook called "Investments" by Bodie, Kane and
Marcus, which tabulates the %ROI and %STDEV of some investments as an
example, and gives references to handbooks containing more data.

This kind of analysis gets really interesting when you start thinking about
portfolios of investments rater than just analyzing them one at a time. 
One standard trick is to combine two high %STDEV investments into one with
lower %STDEV than either.  Perhaps Dani will explain this in a future
investment letter.

